  "question": "A bank pays 100% a year on £1. What if it paid half that every six months, or a twelfth every month?",
  "answer": "More, but never past £2.72. Twice a year gives 1.5 × 1.5 = £2.25; monthly, about £2.61; daily, about £2.71. Jacob Bernoulli posed this in 1683 and showed the limit lies between 2 and 3. It is e, 2.718…, the number behind all continuous growth.",
  "move": "Compounding more often always helps, but the gains shrink towards a limit.",
  "ask": "Which offer of more frequent compounding have I valued above its real worth?",
